{"id":"dag","ord":"a0","title":"DAG","desc":"Workflow TEMPLATES — the pipeline a lane walks, defined ONCE as a subtree.\n\n- Each step is a `todo` node; dependencies are `after` edges.\n- The app draws blocked-vs-ready, so the template is its own diagram.\n- `set_doing` refusing on a blocked node makes the order a refusal rather than a discipline.\n- **LAW:** never mark anything here.\n  - **A DISPATCH IS THE STEP-CAPTURE (the human, 2026-08-29, approved): the FIRST board write of any dispatch is the `add_node` that lands the step subtree — with the fresh item in one call, or UNDER the existing node as its own call. Writing `kolu-terminal`/`dispatched` props or `set_doing` on a lane whose steps do not exist yet is itself the defect.** The order is the checklist; the two dispatch paths (fresh item, existing node) are one shape. (Provenance: all three flat lanes of 2026-08-29 were existing-node dispatches; all five step-cloned lanes were fresh items — the law held only where the tool shape carried it.)\n  - **THAT FIRST WRITE IS ONE APPLY — THE STEPS AND THE DAY-BOARD MIRROR TOGETHER (the human, 2026-08-31, approved): nothing spawns until it returns.** A mirror written \"after\" is a mirror not written: the day board is what the doorbell derives its set from, so a lane the board does not carry is invisible to every doorbell and unsupervised while it works. All-or-nothing in one call, the way the step subtree already is. (Provenance: 2026-08-31 — an odu lane was dispatched, claimed and working with no mirror, because the lane node already existed, the apply that captured its steps was written small, and the mirror had no place left in the sequence.)\n  - **A SPAWN'S ID HAS ONE LEGAL USE — the step's `kolu-terminal` write (the human, 2026-08-31, approved): every terminal order is ADDRESSED FROM THE BOARD — the orchestrator reads the step's (or lane's) `kolu-terminal` prop and sends to that id, never to a remembered spawn result. An unclaimed terminal is one the orchestrator cannot speak to, so a forgotten claim self-arrests at the very next order instead of lying silent.** (Provenance: 2026-08-31 — a reviewer split worked unclaimed because the id was born between the two board writes and the second write was remembered, not forced; the false log entry that followed asserted a prop never read back.)\n  - **A STEP GOES `doing` IN THE SAME APPLY AS THE SPAWN THAT FULFILS IT — ONE LANE AT A TIME (the human, 2026-08-31, approved).** Never flip a step to `doing` for a spawn you have not made in that same beat, and never batch two lanes' transitions into one apply: a mark that promises work, with the act that fulfils it left to memory, is the same defect as an unclaimed terminal one floor up. The board must never claim work nobody is doing. (Provenance: 2026-08-31 — #452's two review steps were flipped `doing` in the same apply as #450's; #450's reviewers were then spawned, the batch felt handled, and #452's reviewers were never dispatched. The doorbell caught it by waking on a step whose terminal was idle.)\n  - Then rewrites the `after` edges onto the clone's ids with `set_after` (or `waitsOn` at capture, which needs no rewrite).\n  - Capture children cannot carry edges yet — superseded: `waitsOn` at capture carries them.\n- **EVERY dispatched lane clones the steps — foreign repos included** (the human, 2026-08-29 ~13:18, approved): a flat lane node is a DEFECT; the `ci` step node existing is what makes \"who runs CI\" a board fact rather than the orchestrator's judgment call.\n- **Step ids:** `dag-<template>-<step>`; a clone keeps the `<step>` suffixes under a `lane-<task>-` prefix, so mark-flipping needs no search.\n- **Edge rule** the template must encode: a step that MUTATES the worktree sits `after` every step that READS it — readers fan out, writers wait.","created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-31T18:25:36-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr","parent":"dag","ord":"a0","title":"pr — implement through merge","desc":"The default pipeline for a dispatched PR lane. Facts the clone carries as properties: `agent`/`repo`/`item` on the lane node, `kolu-terminal` on implement, `pr` on the step that opened it.","created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-31T10:10:19-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-implement","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a0","title":"implement + open PR","todo":true,"desc":"Dispatch IS `set_doing` here. The clone's copy carries `kolu-terminal` as a property; `pr-url` lands when the PR opens.\n\nSPAWN SHAPE (graduated from instructions.md, 2026-08-25): the author spawns as a TOPLEVEL kolu terminal in a fresh worktree under `.worktrees/<name>`, in YOLO mode (`--dangerously-skip-permissions` for Claude), the agent chosen from the roster (agents-root; a name off it is not a choice). Everything through kolu — the discipline on Supervision is binding. Reviewers spawn as SPLITS, per their steps.\n\nTHE STAGE'S LAW (graduated from instructions.md — ir-pipeline-prose, 2026-08-25; CI sentence re-ruled 2026-08-29):\n- The author opens the PR with its initial implementation and NEVER merges it.\n- **REPRODUCE-RED-FIRST FOR EVERY BUG LANE (the human, 2026-09-01, approved): a bug fix begins with a FAILING test that reproduces the bug — written first, RUN, and seen RED against the unfixed code — and only then the fix, which turns that same test green.** The red is the proof the test captured the ACTUAL bug and not an adjacent one; a fix whose test was never seen failing has proven nothing. The PR body states the test was seen red before the fix. If the test will not go red first, the bug is not reproduced — the author STOPS and asks rather than fixing blind. (Provenance: the doorbell-missing-claim P1 — a supervision bug that a reproduction test pins against regression forever.)\n- The author's bar at REPORT time is the LOCAL suites — typecheck, unit, the touched features, run serially and light. Never full CI at implement: CI runs ONCE, at the close step, by the CI WATCHER the orchestrator spawns after the review folds (dag-pr-ci carries the 2026-08-29 re-ruling; the verdict is always the orchestrator's read of the GitHub statuses, never an agent's claim).\n- **AUTHORS NEVER RUN THE E2E SUITE (the human, 2026-08-29 ~19:14) — exactly as they never run CI.** A full parallel e2e on the loaded dev box is a flake generator, not a signal: the toggle lane's full run answered 48 phantom reds and cost ~70 minutes of triage plus two context compactions. ONE-OFF e2e tests — the single feature or scenario the change touches — are fine and expected. The clean-room full run is the CI watcher's, at close, on a quiet venue.\n- The report ends at the PR URL @ head, and the author STOPS — no commissioning reviews, no merging, no board writes, kill by PID only.\n\n*(The capture-the-whole-run rule of 2026-09-01 — every suite run piped to a file first — was REVERTED by the human 2026-09-02; briefs no longer carry it.)*\n\n**DESIGN DECISIONS ARE LEDGERED — TWO KINDS ONLY (the human, 2026-08-29 ~17:28; scope narrowed on their word ~20:15):** an agent MAY settle a design question its brief left open — but never silently. The PR body carries a `## Design decisions` section beside `## Deferrals` — one entry per judgment call the design docs did not settle (what was decided, and why); that full section is for REVIEWERS. What the orchestrator copies into `_olai/Inbox.olai`'s `design decisions` queue for the HUMAN is only: **(1) decisions with USER-FACING impact** (what the user sees, how it behaves, where their data/config lives) **and (2) creation or non-creation of `packages/*`**. Lower-level calls never reach the Inbox. Review is asynchronous — never a merge gate; an entry leaves when the human rules (ratified → trashed; overturned → a lane). A body with no such section claims the design docs settled everything it did.","created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-09-02T10:03:13-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-refactor","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a1","title":"refactor passes","todo":true,"desc":"Only for a non-trivial, non-test-only code change — otherwise SKIPPED.\n\n- The author refactors its own PR, pushing each step as an isolated commit.\n- **Method:**\n  - architecture-first-principles (kolu's skill: .agents/skills/architecture-first-principles/SKILL.md)\n  - hickey (srid/agency .apm/skills/hickey) and lowy (.apm/skills/lowy) *together*, using human intuition so as to keep architecture simple\n  - /simplify when the author is Claude\n- These are method, not ordered sub-steps — one step here, deliberately.","after":["dag-pr-implement"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T16:04:21-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-merge-master","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a4","title":"merge latest master into the PR","todo":true,"desc":"Runs ALWAYS, exactly once, immediately before the CI watcher is dispatched (the human, 2026-08-29, superseding the conflicts-only rule): the AUTHOR merges latest master into the PR — a MERGE COMMIT, never a rebase — so the watcher's run tests the code as it will actually land, and a semantic conflict surfaces as a red in CI rather than at merge time. On a later watcher round (a re-run after review fixes) the author repeats this only if master has moved; a would-be-empty merge is skipped. Sits after both reviews because it mutates the worktree they read — readers fan out, writers wait. Conflicts found here are the author's to resolve inside the same merge. Conflict hygiene stays continuous besides: the orchestrator checks the PR still merges cleanly when the author finishes and again after review fixes — never discovered at merge time.","after":["dag-pr-review-a","dag-pr-review-b"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:58:22-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-address","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a5","title":"address findings","todo":true,"desc":"The author addresses the folded findings, given the PR comment link. (The after-edges to the review steps stand: under the working-state marking, reviews are `done` — idle — by the time this dispatches, so the order is lawful and the edge keeps meaning \"no addressing before the verdicts are in\".) No deferrals — the gate below owns the law; a finding that genuinely cannot be addressed here is a STOP-AND-ASK in the author's terminal, never a note in the PR.","after":["dag-pr-review-a","dag-pr-review-b"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T15:36:05-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-ci","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a6","title":"CI green at head","todo":true,"desc":"CI runs ONCE per PR in the happy path, after the review folds — and THE ORCHESTRATOR RUNS IT ITSELF VIA THE ODU PLUGIN (the human's ban, 2026-09-01 night: \"You are banned from using claude-ci agent for CI. Use odu plugin\" — superseding the 2026-08-29 dedicated-watcher ruling): at close the orchestrator launches the run per odu's SKILL.md read in FULL (venue rulings bind from Supervision's ci-venue child; ONE venue per commit-status context) and follows it through the run's own faces — the odu MCP tools when the conversation carries them, the CLI (`nix run github:juspay/odu`, backgrounded) until odu-baked-in lands the tools everywhere — NEVER a blind sleep. **THE PR IS OPEN BEFORE ANY ODU RUN (the human, 2026-09-02):** an odu run belongs to a PR — a lane whose work loops CI (a shakeout) opens its PR before the first run, because the board's run-attribution and the doorbell's coverage both key off the lane's `pr-url` and a run before the PR exists went unwatched once (the four silent settles; #467 softened the failure, the norm stands). THE CI-RUNS DOORBELL IS THE REPORTING CHANNEL: it rings the scoped conversation at first-red and at settle, and the orchestrator reads the red node's log itself for the failing tests. Two lists still come out of every settled run, now derived by the orchestrator:\n\n- **FLAKES** — every test that failed at ANY point and passed on retry, read from the run's logs and re-run verdicts. Each is ledgered under `_olai/Inbox.olai`'s top-level `flaky tests` node and the flake policy below acts.\n- **REDS** — each genuinely failing recipe with its log path. The orchestrator routes these to the AUTHOR; the fix re-runs at the new head (`node_rerun`/`odu rerun` for one lane on the live run, a fresh run for a new commit) — that run is the price of the finding.\n\n**THE LIVE CHIP IS THE FIRST INSTRUMENT, AND ITS HOME IS THE CI STEP (the human, 2026-08-29 ~20:43 and ~20:55, both proven on live runs):** the board draws every live run as a chip + matrix wherever a row carries a declared `odu-worktree` (resolved via `pr-url` on the SAME row) — so **when the ci step goes doing, the orchestrator WRITES `odu-worktree` + `pr-url` on the ci step node** (the lane's own copies stay the facts of record); the chip then draws exactly where a person hunts for CI. No product change was needed — the seam's key-selects design carried it (tested live on #437's run, 20:55). The orchestrator's FIRST read of a run's state is the BOARD's chip (screenshot the deployed board when reading programmatically). A dispatched lane missing its `odu-worktree` prop is a defect (its runs can't be seen).\n\n**THE WATCHER SEAT IS GONE (the human, 2026-09-01, ending the 2026-08-29 interim seat):** no claude-ci is spawned, in any repo; the roster row stands retired as history and orchestrator/ci-watcher.md is deleted. **GITHUB-NATIVE CI is likewise the orchestrator's own watch** (`gh pr checks`, `gh run watch`, `gh run rerun` on its own judgment — it never launches what launches itself). **THE AUTHOR STILL NEVER TOUCHES CI IN ANY REPO** — there is no repo shape where CI routes to the author, and an orchestrator order that would send CI work to an author is itself the defect.\n\n**THE VERDICT IS NEVER AN AGENT'S CLAIM** (the provenance stands: an author once reported `just check`-local as \"CI green\") — the orchestrator reads the machine truth: `gh pr checks <n> --required` must show an explicit pass on every required check, or mergeStateStatus == CLEAN. Never judge from the check rollup.\n\n**THE FLAKE POLICY** (the human, 2026-08-25; re-homed here 2026-08-29 so the boot chain carries the law — the Inbox node is the LEDGER, this is the LAW): a reported flake the PR does not own is filed one-entry-per-test-file under the `flaky tests` node and dispatched IMMEDIATELY — no triage lag. Several open at once = ONE lane fixes them all in a SINGLE PR. The author is pi or grok, never Opus. Auto-merge on review folded + CI green (the standing flake-class grant).\n\n**CodeQL ALERTS are branch-protection physics** (the human, 2026-08-26 ~19:46): ruleset 20608873 carries a `code_scanning` rule — ANY open CodeQL alert, any severity, blocks the merge — beside the ten required contexts; `gh pr checks --required` + mergeStateStatus is the whole gate.","after":["dag-pr-merge-master","dag-pr-address"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-09-02T08:59:09-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-evidence","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a7","title":"evidence verified","todo":true,"desc":"The author produces a screenshot (or video) proving the change working; the ORCHESTRATOR frame-verifies it before the PR is fielded. Evidence for terminal/visual work is photographed against REAL data (a busy stream, live padi) — quiet fixtures do not count as evidence.\n\nTHE RECIPE (moved here from olai's CLAUDE.md on the human's word, 2026-08-29 — evidence tooling is the orchestrator's to teach, not the downstream repo's): produce with a throwaway section at `.saatchi/evidence.ts` + `nix run github:juspay/saatchi` (shots land in `.saatchi/shots/`); upload with `nix run github:juspay/saatchi#publish` (paste-ready markdown block, video transcode included); details in saatchi's README — the recipe's one true source. Non-media artifacts or endpoint failure: Crabbox artifact publishing plus the manifest URL. NEVER push proof assets to any product repo branch; never commit `.github/pr-assets`.","after":["dag-pr-ci"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:41:05-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-deferrals","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a8","title":"deferrals ruled #human","todo":true,"desc":"HUMAN gate: a PR whose report carries deferrals merges only on the human's explicit word — **each deferral becomes a child of the `Deferrals` node in `_olai/Inbox.olai` first, carrying a `pr-url` prop naming the PR that introduced it** (the human's rulings 2026-08-28: deferrals park under the Inbox's Deferrals parent, never scattered across roadmap files, and every one links back to its source PR). A report that says \"no deferrals\" in so many words skips this gate.\n\nTHE LAW: a PR ships everything its roadmap item names and everything it spots in the code it touches — refactors of touched code, pre-existing hacks, review findings, nits. Diff size, noise, review burden, \"different shape\", \"wants its own PR\" are never reasons; neither is another repository's gate or a design question. What genuinely cannot be done in the PR is a STOP-AND-ASK in the author's terminal, and the orchestrator puts it to the human before the PR moves (overnight autonomy excepted). A `## Deferrals` section saying anything but `No deferrals.` is not merge-ready; \"filed as a follow-up\" counts as a deferral.","after":["dag-pr-evidence"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-28T19:16:11-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-merge","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a9","title":"merge","todo":true,"desc":"AUTO-MERGE IS THE DEFAULT: the pipeline completing — reviews folded, CI green at head (the ci step's explicit-pass bar), visual evidence frame-verified where the lane produces it — IS the merge authorization; the orchestrator squash-merges without a further ask. `merge-human` is the exception a ruling marks on the lane, honored absolutely.\n\n- Conflicts → the lane's author resolves them, then retry.\n- Multiple merges land in the order minimizing conflict work.\n- Before merging (or fielding a human-marked lane): read the author's FINAL message in its terminal; anything left \"for you\" is a STOP-AND-ASK, not a note.\n- Never file issues or act on another repo without the human's ratification. Only a URL or a roadmap entry counts as tracked.","after":["dag-pr-deferrals"],"created":"2026-08-15T19:05:46-04:00","changed":"2026-08-26T19:49:56-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-retire","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"aA","title":"retire terminals","todo":true,"desc":"Kill EVERY terminal the lane spawned — the author too — and ONLY after the merge is confirmed, NEVER before: a green, reported, human-gated PR is still an OPEN lane, and a conflict round needs the author's live context, not a cold respawn. Applies to every lane shape. Reviewers MAY retire earlier, once their verdict is folded. **THE CI WATCHER SEAT IS GONE** (the human, 2026-09-01, superseding the 2026-08-29 never-early-retirable law): CI is the orchestrator's own via the odu plugin, so there is no watcher to retire.\n\n- Verified against the live terminal list; executed in the SAME action block as the merge, never deferred.\n- Worktree removal is a SEPARATE step after the merge is confirmed — never bundled into the merge command.\n- After the merge the orchestrator syncs its own $PWD: `git pull --ff-only` (or `git pull --rebase` when local board commits predate the merge).","after":["dag-pr-merge"],"created":"2026-08-17T19:20:10-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T20:18:25-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-review-a","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a1V","title":"review: first reviewer — named from the roster at dispatch","todo":true,"desc":"WHO reviews is never written here: the menu is the roster (agents-root's children; the clone's `reviewer` ref prop takes the choice, its step title takes the name — this template holds only the role).\n\nTHE MARK IS THE REVIEWER'S WORKING STATE (the human, 2026-08-29, superseding the same-day never-closes draft): `doing` whenever the reviewer is WORKING — the first read, and every re-verdict round; `done` when it is idle with nothing outstanding (verdict posted, or cleared). **A re-dispatch is a dispatch: the step goes back to `doing` in the same beat as — and BEFORE — the terminal order that starts the work. A work order without its board write is the defect** (provenance: 2026-08-29 15:28, two reviewers re-verdicting with zero doing nodes — the re-verdict order was a stateless act the sweep could not see). Retirement is retire-step law.\n\nTHE STAGE'S LAW:\n- The reviewer reviews per HACKING.md and posts ONE PR comment — verdict + MUST/SHOULD/NIT at file:line; re-verdicts reply on the same thread.\n- Reviewers READ CODE: no suite runs, no CI, one surgical probe max, never edit/push/merge — the reviewer contract is binding; gate verification is the orchestrator's.\n- Never two agents WRITING the same files at once; reviews are reads — fanning out is safe.\n\nSPAWN SHAPE: a reviewer opens as a SPLIT of the author's terminal, cwd the author's worktree — never top-level.","after":["dag-pr-refactor"],"created":"2026-08-25T15:59:19-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T15:35:56-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-review-b","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"a1l","title":"review: second reviewer — named from the roster at dispatch (skipped when the lane names one)","todo":true,"desc":"Same law and spawn shape as the first review step — the roster names who, the clone's title takes the name, this template holds the role. THE MARK IS THE REVIEWER'S WORKING STATE (the human, 2026-08-29): doing while working (first read and every re-verdict round — the step flips BEFORE the order goes to the terminal), done while idle with nothing outstanding. A lane ruled single-reviewer clones one review step; pi-authored PRs clone BOTH (grok + Claude Opus).","after":["dag-pr-refactor"],"created":"2026-08-25T15:59:25-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T15:35:58-04:00"}
{"id":"dag-pr-timings","parent":"dag-pr","ord":"aB","title":"timings posted","todo":true,"desc":"THE ORCHESTRATOR'S OWN CLOSING ACT (the human, 2026-08-29): after retire, compute each pipeline step's duration and post the DAG timings as ONE PR comment; the same summary line lands on the lane node as a `timings` prop, for posterity. No agent runs this step — it is a reading of the board's own instants: the lane's `dispatched` prop opens the window, each step's `done` instant closes its span (a step's duration = its done minus the previous step's done; the two review steps run in parallel and share one window, said as one row). Format: a small table, step · AGENT · duration (Xm Ys), ending with the dispatch→merge TOTAL — every row names who did it (the lane's `agent` for author steps, each review row's `reviewer`, the CI watcher's seat, the orchestrator for the close). Skipped steps get a dash, not a zero.\n\nTHE CI ROW GETS A SECOND TABLE (the human, same ruling): the odu run's own per-node timings — recipe · duration, per platform when both ran — read from the commit statuses' descriptions (odu writes \"Succeeded (4m29s): …\" per context), so the pipeline-step number and the machine's own breakdown sit side by side.\n\nTHE QUERYABLE LAYER (the human, 2026-08-29 ~12:35; re-founded on #430 with the human's ok, ~17:35): the NATIVE fields are the source — `set_doing` at the true start (the dispatch law already mandates it) stamps `started`, the settle closes it, and `took` is DERIVED at read time, never stored. The orchestrator writes a `took` PROP (whole seconds, the typed key in _olai/Properties.olai) ONLY as back-fill where the native span cannot exist: pre-#430 history, todo→done jumps. Two tooks on one node is the defect the fence exists for. Agent-performance questions are board queries: took × the step id's suffix × the lane's agent/tier; the timings table reads each step's native `took` where present (the step's own working span — queue gaps stay visible between rows instead of smeared in). The quality axis rides on review steps as `musts`/`shoulds` counts, written when the verdict folds. Scope added mid-round (a ruling landing in an address window) is named in the lane's desc so a long span is not misread as a slow agent.","after":["dag-pr-retire"],"created":"2026-08-29T12:31:03-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T17:33:45-04:00"}
